<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1556–1707 (Akbar to Aurangzeb)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Emperor to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari.jpg" alt="Ain-i-Akbari"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari">Ain-i-Akbari</a><span class="soc-credit">Basawan · Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> ranked people by their place in the emperor’s service, by their rights over land and by birth. The emperor granted every rank in an imperial service that was army, nobility and government at once: the mansabdars, a deliberately mixed elite of Central Asians, Iranians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, Indian Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>, paid with assignments of land revenue that he moved at will. Below them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> held hereditary rights over the revenue of the countryside, from Rajput kings to village landholders, and religion gave its own honour to Muslim scholars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> shaikhs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests. The great majority were peasants, who owed a third or more of their harvest to the state, and society was divided into many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, the lowest held to be untouchable. The ladder was steep: around 1650 the 445 highest-ranked nobles drew 61 per cent of the empire’s revenue.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1556–1707 (Akbar to Aurangzeb)</span><span><b>People</b> 116–150 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 9</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Pādshāh</span> <span class="soc-en">(The emperor)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:39%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Mansabdār</span> <span class="soc-en">(Ranked nobles and officers)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:54%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Zamīndār</span> <span class="soc-en">(Landholders and chiefs)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–2% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Ulamā</span> <span class="soc-en">(Religious scholars and holy men)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–2% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:70%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:3%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Sarrāf</span> <span class="soc-en">(Merchants and bankers)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 2–4% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:85%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:12%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Artisans and townspeople</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 10–15% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:69%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Raiyat</span> <span class="soc-en">(Peasants)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 69% (the rest, est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-outcast soc-est" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:11%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">The lowest castes</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 8–14% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-est" href="#rank-9" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Ghulām</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/akbar" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akbar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Pādshāh</span> <span class="soc-en">(The emperor)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The Mughal emperor gave every noble his rank, and when he died his sons often fought each other for the throne.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The emperor, the pādshāh, was the source of every rank. He granted, raised and cut the mansabs of the nobility and assigned and withdrew the revenue that paid them, and when a noble died his estate came under the emperor’s control, the heirs receiving what he chose to give them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> divided the power of a single chief minister among four, for finance, the army, the household and religious grants, so that none could dominate. In law the emperor was the final judge: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> hung a “chain of justice” in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> Fort that any wronged subject could shake. The revenue of the crown lands, the khalisa, which was never assigned to nobles, kept his household and paid his own troops. No rule fixed the succession. Princes held ranks and armies of their own and fought for the throne: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> defeated his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shikoh</a> in 1659 and kept their father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> a prisoner until his death in 1666.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Only a prince of the dynasty, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, could reign, and the throne went to the son who won the struggle that followed an emperor’s illness or death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> came to it in 1556, aged thirteen, under the regent Bairam Khan.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with the imperial family and the women of the imperial household.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peacock-throne">Peacock Throne</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-fort">Red Fort</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/man-singh-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>Fewer than 1 in 1,000</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Mansabdār</span> <span class="soc-en">(Ranked nobles and officers)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">umarā</p><p class="soc-kid">Mughal nobles held numbered ranks that showed how many horsemen they had to keep ready for the emperor.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansabdari-system">mansabdari</a> system every officer of the empire above the lowest levels, judges apart, held a mansab, a numbered rank in one service that was army, nobility and civil government at once. From about 1595 each rank had two numbers: the zat fixed a man’s pay and precedence, the sawar the horsemen he had to keep. Ranks ran from 10 up to 5,000, later 7,000, for anyone but a prince, and only holders of 500, later 1,000, and above counted as nobles (umara). Lesser officers were often paid in cash; most of the rest held a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a>, the right to collect the land revenue of named villages, which gave no ownership of the land and was moved every three or four years. The mansabdars numbered 1,823 in 1595 and some 8,000 by 1650, when the 445 highest drew 61 per cent of the empire’s revenue. The emperors mixed them on purpose: Turanis from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, Iranis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, Indian Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, Deccanis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A newcomer needed a patron to present him to the paymaster general and money for horses and men; promotions came at festivals, on campaigns or for a handsome present. No rank or title was inherited, but sons of the service, the khanazads, held nearly half the high ranks under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> About 8,000 mansabdars around 1650 (Stephen Blake’s count); with their families, roughly 0.02–0.06 per cent of the population.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Man Singh I</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-rahim-khan-i-khanan">Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-i">Jai Singh I</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansabdari-system">Mansabdari system</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">Jagir</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mughal-empire-3.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 1–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Zamīndār</span> <span class="soc-en">(Landholders and chiefs)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Zamindars were local landholders and chiefs who collected taxes from farmers and kept part for themselves.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> held hereditary rights over the revenue of the land, and the word covered men of very different weight. Mughal chroniclers used it for the rajas who ruled their own territories from forts, two to three hundred of them under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> by one contemporary count, for intermediaries over whole districts, and for landholders with rights in a single village. They collected the land revenue from the peasants for the state and kept a customary share, and were expected to advance loans and tools in hard times. Many commanded armed kinsmen: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari">Ain-i-Akbari</a> puts the zamindars’ forces at 384,558 horsemen and over four million foot, a local militia rather than an army. Most great zamindars were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> of high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayastha">Kayasthas</a>, but there were Jat and Muslim zamindars too, and Rajput kings who entered the imperial service kept their ancestral lands as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagirs</a>. Under too heavy a demand, zamindars took up arms.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">Zamindari</a> rights passed by inheritance, though the choice of heir depended in part on the emperor’s pleasure. A chief who submitted might be given a mansab; in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a> accepted only a nominal Mughal overlordship and kept his autonomy.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the zamindars, great and small, with their families; Arif Qandhari counted two to three hundred autonomous chiefs under Akbar alone.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">Zamindar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A Rajput nobleman on horseback, painting from Rajasthan, early 18th century (Metropolitan Museum of Art) · Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Equestrian_Portrait_of_a_Nobleman_-_2002.411_-_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-4"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/ulama" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulama.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>c. 1–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Ulamā</span> <span class="soc-en">(Religious scholars and holy men)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">qāzī, shaikh, brāhmaṇ</p><p class="soc-kid">Muslim scholars and judges, Sufi holy men and Hindu priests called Brahmins were honoured for their learning.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Learning and holiness formed a rank of their own in both great faiths. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, scholars of Islamic law, sat as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">qazis</a>, from the district judge to the chief qazi who travelled with the imperial camp, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> shaikhs kept shrines that emperors themselves visited: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> prayed at the tomb of Moinuddin Chishti at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a> beside the shrine of Salim Chishti. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests and scholars performed the rites of temple and household and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning alive; the Brahmin poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> retold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> in Awadhi. A minister, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadr">sadr</a>, managed the madad-i-ma’ash, tax-free grants of land revenue to the learned and the pious, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> gave many more than Akbar. Favour followed policy: Akbar’s House of Worship, built in 1575, came to host debates among men of every faith, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> had 500 jurists compile the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatawa-i-alamgiri">Fatawa-i-Alamgiri</a> and in 1672–73 ordered the grants held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> taken back.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Muslim scholars rose by learning and patronage, and a trained jurist could be appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">qazi</a> by the emperor or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadr">sadr</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> rank came by birth, and some Brahmins lived as landholders, officials or farmers instead.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the Muslim scholars and judges, the Sufi shaikhs and the Brahmin priests, with their families.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">Qazi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatawa-i-alamgiri">Fatawa-i-Alamgiri</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mughal-empire-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 2–4% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Sarrāf</span> <span class="soc-en">(Merchants and bankers)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Mughal merchants and bankers grew rich from trade and sent money between distant cities using written bills.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The great merchants and bankers were rich, and the empire’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> economy ran through their hands. Long-distance trade lay largely with communities such as the Banias, Marwaris and Khatris, the Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohras">Bohras</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a>. Sarrafs, money-changers and bankers, issued hundis, bills of exchange that could be cashed in distant cities. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a>, the empire’s great port, Virji Vora traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, bullion, coral and opium through agents from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>, lent to the English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> companies, and was said by the English to be worth 8 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. Wealth brought no rank in the imperial order, however. In 1638 the governor of Surat jailed Virji Vora until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> summoned him to court; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> taxed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> merchants at 5 per cent against 2.5 for Muslims; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>’s raid on Surat in 1664 plundered Virji Vora’s house and warehouses.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Trade and banking ran in families and communities. A few merchants crossed into the imperial service, as the Persian diamond trader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jumla-ii">Mir Jumla II</a> did, rising to be a Mughal general and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>; extortion by officials or a raid could ruin a house.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the merchant and banking families, among them the Bania castes, the Bohras and the Khatris.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohras">Bohras</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Anoopchund, a shroff (banker) of Gujarat, by Robert Melville Grindlay, 1809 · Robert Melville Grindlay · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Melville_Grindlay_(1786-1877)_-_Anoopchund_Shroff_or_Banker_of_Kurrie%2C_Guzerat_-_03546(IS)_-_Victoria_and_Albert_Museum.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mughal-empire-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>c. 10–15% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Artisans and townspeople</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Mughal weavers made fine cotton cloth, such as muslin, that people bought all over Asia and Europe.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> had many large towns: under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, Nizamuddin Ahmad counted 120 great cities and 3,200 townships, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> may each have held several hundred thousand people. Their artisans, shopkeepers, moneylenders and labourers lived by the court and the market. Weaving was the greatest industry: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cottons</a>, calicoes and muslins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and other provinces sold across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and in the early 18th century India held a quarter of the world’s trade in textiles. In the emperor’s karkhanas, or workshops, craftsmen made arms, clothes, jewellery, carpets and tents for the court. Most crafts belonged to particular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jati">jatis</a>, hereditary groups that married among themselves. Wages for manual labour were low, and a town’s fortunes rose and fell with the court: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>’s new capital at Delhi, Shahjahanabad, completed in 1648, drew people to it.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Most artisans followed the craft of their family and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jati">jati</a>. The most skilled could find work in the imperial workshops, and people moved to wherever the court and its demand went.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: estimates of the share of people living in towns around 1600 run from under 9 per cent (Tim Dyson) to about 15 per cent (Irfan Habib, Shireen Moosvi), and many artisans lived in villages.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Masons and labourers build Fatehpur Sikri under Akbar's eye: painting by Balchand for the Akbarnama, 1603–05 (Chester Beatty) · Balchand · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balchand._Akbar_inspecting_construction_for_his_new_capital_Fatehpur_Sikri%2C_Akbarnama%2C_1603-5%2C_Chester_Beatty_Library.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mughal-empire-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 69% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Raiyat</span> <span class="soc-en">(Peasants)</span></h3><p class="soc-term"><span class="soc-nw">khud-kāsht,</span> <span class="soc-nw">pāhī-kāsht</span></p><p class="soc-kid">Most people in Mughal India were farmers who grew wheat, rice and cotton and paid heavy taxes on their harvests.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The peasants, the raiyat, were the great majority and paid for the empire. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabt-system">zabt</a> system worked out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a> from 1580, fields in the core provinces were measured and the state’s demand fixed in cash at a third of the average crop of the previous ten years; elsewhere the crop was shared or assessed village by village. In practice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irfan-habib">Irfan Habib</a> argued, added cesses and assignees who held their villages for only a few years had pushed the demand to half the crop or more in many places by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>’s reign. Resident cultivators, the khud-kasht, had a hereditary right to till their fields as long as they paid; the pahi-kasht farmed in other villages at lower rates. Peasants grew wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and barley to eat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, sugar cane and opium for sale, and took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and tobacco from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> between 1600 and 1650. When the burden grew too heavy they fled or rose, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> did in 1669.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Clearing new land earned a peasant tax-free years; a heavy demand could drive a family from its fields, and revenue officers could even enslave peasants who failed to pay.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other tiers: the great majority (Shireen Moosvi reckons 82 per cent of the late-16th-century labour force rural). Several of the other ranks are estimates, so this share is one too.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabt-system">Zabt system</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A village in the Punjab, painting by Ghulam Ali Khan, a Delhi artist of the late Mughal period, early 19th century · Ghulam Ali Khan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Village_Scene_In_The_Punjab_by_Gulam_Ali_Khan.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-outcast" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mughal-empire-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Outcast groups</span> · <span>c. 8–14% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">The lowest castes</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">People of the lowest castes were kept apart by higher castes and did work such as tanning leather and sweeping.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Below the peasants stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> texts placed outside the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> altogether and that higher castes treated as untouchable, because their hereditary work, such as tanning hides and sweeping, was counted as polluting. The four varnas were a scheme of the texts; lived society was made of many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jati">jatis</a>, groups born to an occupation that married among themselves, and historians debate how rigid the order was before British rule, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> and laws hardened it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irfan-habib">Irfan Habib</a> argued that custom kept the lowest castes from holding land, so that they worked other men’s fields and served as village menials. They were not voiceless. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a>, a poet-saint born to a leather-working family, preached against caste, and his verses entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> scripture, the Adi Granth. In 1672 the Satnamis of Narnaul, a sect that rejected caste and drew many followers from low-caste leatherworkers, rose against Mughal officials; the court chronicler sneered at them as goldsmiths, carpenters, sweepers and tanners.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> came by birth. Devotional movements that rejected caste, like those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a> and the Satnamis, offered dignity and community, though not a higher rank.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the castes then called “depressed” were about 14 per cent of the people of British India in the census of 1931; the share in the 17th century was probably of the same order.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Chamars, a caste of leather-workers, cutting leather and making shoes: photograph, late 19th or early 20th century · Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photograph_of_Chamars_with_caption_'Chamars_cutting_leather_and_making_shoes'.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-9"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/mughal-empire-9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Ghulām</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay, mostly as servants in rich households.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> in Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was mostly domestic. Enslaved men and women served in the households of the emperor and the nobles as servants, concubines and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, and were given as gifts and dowries: when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Prince Salim</a> married the daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> raja Bhagwant Das, her dowry included male and female slaves of Abyssinian, Caucasian and Indian origin. People were enslaved in war, when the women and children of defeated rebels were taken and shared among officers, and through the revenue system: a missionary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1629–30 reported that revenue officers could enslave peasants who failed to pay. Others were bought, among them Ethiopians, called Habshis, prized as soldiers and servants, while Indian captives were sold north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatawa-i-alamgiri">Fatawa-i-Alamgiri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>’s code of Islamic law, denied slaves inheritance and the right to testify and required their owner’s leave to marry, but it also set out how they could be freed.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom came by manumission, in whole or in part, under rules the jurists set out. Slave soldiers could rise far: in the neighbouring Ahmadnagar Sultanate the Ethiopian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a>, brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as a slave, became regent and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>’s resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: slavery was mostly domestic, in the households of nobles and the well-off; famines and wars also drove people into bondage.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">Ghulam</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">Eunuch</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: An African man with a long staff, Indian painting, 17th century: Habshis, Ethiopians bought as slaves, were prized as soldiers and servants · Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Black_Man_Leans_on_a_Long_Staff_(6125084816).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="mughal-empire">Be born in Mughal India</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Mughal society was steep, but its summit was open to talent, fortune and the emperor’s favour. No rank in the imperial service was hereditary, and emperors kept recruiting new men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a> began as a clerk and rose to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>’s finance minister with a rank of 4,000. Mirza Ghiyas Beg reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from Iran impoverished and held only a modest post until his daughter married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> in 1611 as Nur Jahan; he then became chief minister. The Persian diamond merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jumla-ii">Mir Jumla II</a> became a Mughal general and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> chiefs entered the nobility by alliance, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwahas</a> of Amber from 1562, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> won over Deccani nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> chiefs with high ranks. Yet sons of the service held nearly half the high ranks under Aurangzeb, and below the elite change was slow: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> kept their crafts, peasants could win tax-free years by clearing new land, and a heavy revenue demand drove families to flight or revolt.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>Women’s lives followed the rank of their families. Some women of the imperial household held great power and wealth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>’s wife Mariam-uz-Zamani owned trading ships; Nur Jahan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>’s empress from 1611, held the imperial seal, had coins struck in her name, traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and cloth, and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and her father’s tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>’s daughter Jahanara held the revenues of the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a>, owned ships and laid out Chandni Chowk in Shahjahanabad. Islamic law gave a Muslim daughter half the inheritance of a son and let women own property in their own right. Among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, sati, a widow’s burning on her husband’s pyre, was practised in some families, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> ordered it banned in 1663. Peasant and artisan women worked alongside their families, and enslaved women were at the disposal of their owners.</p></section>
