<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Athar Ali, M. (1966) The Mughal Nobility under Aurangzeb. Bombay: Asia Publishing House.</li><li>Bayly, S. (1999) Caste, Society and Politics in India from the Eighteenth Century to the Modern Age. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Dyson, T. (2018) A Population History of India: From the First Modern People to the Present Day. Oxford: Oxford University Press.</li><li>Habib, I. (1999) The Agrarian System of Mughal India, 1556–1707. 2nd edn. New Delhi: Oxford University Press.</li><li>Raychaudhuri, T. and Habib, I. (eds) (1982) The Cambridge Economic History of India, Volume 1: c. 1200–c. 1750. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Richards, J. F. (1993) The Mughal Empire. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: modern estimates for India around 1600: Tim Dyson puts the serious assessments for about 1595 at 116–145 million, and some run to 150 million.</p></section>
